WEBVTT

00:50:58.021 --> 00:51:01.234
and I think she's correctly identified
the key trait of our age.

00:51:01.258 --> 00:51:03.629
CA: Yeah, she had a memorable TED Talk

00:51:03.653 --> 00:51:06.446
that is actually one of
the most viewed of all time.

00:51:06.470 --> 00:51:09.195
And that idea, actually,
really resonated with people.

00:51:09.219 --> 00:51:12.036
There are a lot people, I think,
who are holding tension,

00:51:12.060 --> 00:51:14.310
and by being given permission
to be vulnerable,

00:51:14.334 --> 00:51:16.513
it actually is a big deal for them.

00:51:16.537 --> 00:51:18.892
DB: Yeah, and that is how
a relationship is built.

00:51:18.916 --> 00:51:22.537
It's when one person's vulnerable,
and the other matches their vulnerability

00:51:22.561 --> 00:51:23.722
and then offers.

00:51:23.746 --> 00:51:26.861
And then it's a series of gradual
deepening and trusting gestures.

00:51:26.885 --> 00:51:30.872
CA: I mean, the question for me is --
you know, these stories are inspiring.